WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Over the past 20 years, the field of positive psychology has grown from a fledgling idea to a worldwide movement. Positive psychology is the scientific study of the strengths that enable individuals and communities to thrive. It is based on the idea that psychological research can help us understand the characteristics and qualities that allow people to lead more meaningful and fulfilling lives, and then design techniques to help them do that. After a year when many of us have had a hard time staying optimistic, most of us would welcome any tips on how to become more positive. What does positive psychology have to say about flourishing in tough times, such as a pandemic?
在过去的20年里，积极心理学领域已经从一个萌芽中的想法发展成为一场全球性的运动。积极心理学是对使个人和社区能够茁壮成长的优势的科学研究。它基于这样一种理念：心理学研究可以帮助我们理解那些让人过上更有意义和更充实生活的特征和品质，并据此设计出帮助人们实现这一目标的方法。在许多人难以保持乐观的一年后，我们大多数人都很欢迎关于如何变得更积极的建议。积极心理学对于在疫情等困难时期如何蓬勃发展有什么见解呢？

2
00:00:42.000 --> 00:01:22.000
And for people who are not optimistic by nature, is there anything they can do to become more positive? Does being positive offer particular benefits, such as perhaps greater resistance to viruses? Welcome to speaking of psychology, the flagship. flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. Our guest today is Dr. Martin Seligman, a professor of psychology at the University of Pennsylvania, and director of the Penn Positive Psychology Center. He's been called the founder of Positive Psychology and has published
对于天生不乐观的人来说，他们能做些什么来变得更积极吗？变得积极是否能提供特别的好处，比如可能增强对病毒的抵抗力？欢迎收听美国心理学会的旗舰播客《心理学谈话》，该节目探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯。我们今天的嘉宾是宾夕法尼亚大学心理学教授、宾大积极心理学中心主任马丁·塞利格曼博士。他被称为积极心理学的创始人，并已发表了

3
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:59.000
more than 350 scholarly publications and 30 books, including his latest and autobiography, called the Hope Circuit, which you can find on Amazon. He was also president of the American Psychological Association in 1998, during which one of his presidential initiatives was the promotion of positive psychology as a field of scientific study. He is also a leading authority in the fields of resilience, learned helplessness, depression, optimism, and pessimism. And he is a recognized authority on interventions that prevent depression and build strengths and well-being.
超过350篇学术论文和30本书，包括他最新的自传《希望回路》（The Hope Circuit），你可以在亚马逊上找到。他还是1998年美国心理学会的主席，任职期间，他的总统倡议之一就是推动积极心理学作为一门科学研究领域的发展。他也是韧性、习得性无助、抑郁、乐观和悲观领域的权威专家。同时，他还是预防抑郁、建立优势和幸福感的干预措施方面的公认权威。

4
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Welcome to speaking of psychology, Dr. Seligman. Oh, thank you, Kim. Thanks for having me. Let's start with one of the questions I posed in the introduction. It's been more than a year since the SARS-CoV-2 virus was identified, and while vaccines are providing a good deal of hope, there's still a long winter ahead of us. What lessons can people take from positive psychology that they can use to get through the next few months?
欢迎来到《心理学谈话》，塞利格曼博士。哦，谢谢你，金。谢谢邀请我。让我们从我在介绍中提出的一个问题开始。距离SARS-CoV-2病毒被发现已经过去一年多了，虽然疫苗带来了很大的希望，但我们面前仍然有一个漫长的冬天。人们可以从积极心理学中汲取哪些经验来度过接下来的几个月呢？

5
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Well, positive psychology has a lot to say about COVID and what we should be doing. And I should take him that I'm personally, this is quite a tale for me. We made the mistake of having Thanksgiving with all of our five kids. Good thing to do. We all got tested before. My son came in from Chicago. All of a sudden, he had it. Oh, no.
嗯，积极心理学对新冠疫情以及我们应该做什么有很多见解。我得告诉你，金，对我个人来说，这真是一个传奇故事。我们犯了一个错误，和我们的五个孩子一起过了感恩节。这本是件好事。我们事先都做了检测。我儿子从芝加哥赶来。突然之间，他感染了。哦，不。

6
00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:31.000
And all five of my kids and my wife came down with COVID. whereupon they kicked me out of the house and I evacuated to a hotel for 17 days until they were past the period of recovery. And I just returned about a week ago. And I've been in quarantine since March 1st. And so COVID has been quite an experience for this family. And I expected to be going on through about the spring. So my task is to hold out until I can get the jab. I hope that'll be in March and then we can resume life.
结果我的五个孩子和我的妻子都感染了新冠。于是他们把我赶出了家门，我撤离到一家酒店住了17天，直到他们度过了恢复期。我大约一周前才回来。从3月1日开始我就一直在隔离。所以新冠对我们这个家庭来说真是一次特别的经历。我预计这种情况会持续到春天左右。所以我的任务是坚持到我能打上疫苗。我希望那会在三月份，然后我们就能恢复正常生活了。

7
00:03:31.000 --> 00:04:18.000
But let me say what positive psychology has told us. And I think the science tells people about COVID. First, I think when one talks about positive psychology, it's important to make nuanced distinctions about different kinds of well-being. And the one I'm about to make is the difference between smiling, merry, cheerful, being happy, called positive, affective, and optimistic, forward-looking, which is not a feeling. It's a cognition about the future. It's about hope. Now, those are both positive, but they have different effects, particularly with respect to different illnesses.
但让我说说积极心理学告诉了我们什么。我认为科学告诉了人们关于新冠的什么。首先，我认为当谈论积极心理学时，对不同类型的幸福感做出细致的区分是很重要的。我接下来要做的区分是：微笑、快乐、欢呼、幸福（称为积极情感）与乐观、前瞻性（这不是一种感觉，而是一种关于未来的认知，即希望）之间的区别。现在，这两者都是积极的，但它们有不同的影响，特别是在针对不同疾病方面。

8
00:04:18.000 --> 00:05:03.000
So there is a literature on protection from rhinovirus. A rhinovirus, as you know, is a coronavirus. It's the common cold virus. And Sheldon Cohen in Pittsburgh took a large number of volunteers, paid the meat $300, and put rhinoviruses in their nose, and then isolated them for two weeks and asked, could he predict who would get a cold and how severe it would be? And before they're isolated, he gives them a whole battery of tests, optimism, cheerfulness, positive emotion, negative emotion, and the like.
关于预防鼻病毒的文献确实存在。如你所知，鼻病毒是一种冠状病毒。它是普通感冒病毒。匹兹堡的谢尔顿·科恩招募了大量志愿者，付给他们300美元，把鼻病毒放进他们的鼻子里，然后将他们隔离两周，并询问他是否能预测谁会感冒以及感冒会有多严重？在他们被隔离之前，他给他们做了一整套测试，包括乐观、开朗、积极情绪、消极情绪等等。

9
00:05:03.000 --> 00:06:09.000
The results are quite remarkable and they're substantial. First, interesting to me, since I've spent my life working on optimism, optimism had no effect on whether or not you get a cold and how long it left. The big effect was being cheerful, merry, high, positive, effective before the rhinovirus is put in the nose. Happy people, in the subjective well-being sense, got about half the colds, and they were shorter and less severe. And by the way, this was not about complaining since he actually weighed the mucus. So it's a rigorous, a rigorous measure of getting a cold. So the first lesson is during the pandemic, if you're in a situation like mine in which what you're trying to do for the next three months is just avoid getting it, in addition to wearing a mask and social distancing, you should have as much fun as you possibly can.
结果非常显著且具有实质性。首先，这对我来说很有趣，因为我一生都在研究乐观，但乐观对你是否感冒以及感冒持续多久没有任何影响。最大的影响是在鼻病毒进入鼻子之前是否开朗、快乐、具有高度积极的情感。在主观幸福感意义上快乐的人，感冒的几率大约只有一半，而且感冒时间更短，症状更轻。顺便说一句，这与抱怨无关，因为他实际上称量了粘液。所以这是一个衡量感冒的严谨指标。因此，第一个教训是，在疫情期间，如果你处于像我这样试图在接下来的三个月里尽量避免感染的情况下，除了戴口罩和保持社交距离外，你应该尽可能多地找乐子。

10
00:06:09.000 --> 00:07:04.000
Now, of course, it's difficult in a pandemic to have a lot of fun. But let me tell you my advice and what we've done. First, we bought a puppy. And the puppy brought us until it's now 10 months old, enormous joy. Dancing, singing, sex, making love, good food, all the things that you can do in a pandemic in quarantine to have. have fun, even how difficult it is. So I have Zoom sessions with my students and we dance. And a lot of the positive psychology website people have created COVID exercises to have fun. So that's part one. The science tells us as hard as it is, have as much fun as you can.
当然，在疫情期间要找很多乐子是很困难的。但让我告诉你我的建议以及我们做了什么。首先，我们买了一只小狗。这只小狗直到现在10个月大，给我们带来了巨大的快乐。跳舞、唱歌、性生活、做爱、美食，所有这些你在疫情隔离期间可以做来找乐子的事情，尽管这很难。所以我通过Zoom和我的学生们开会，我们一起跳舞。许多积极心理学网站的人也创造了新冠期间的娱乐练习。所以这是第一部分。科学告诉我们，尽管很难，但要尽可能多地找乐子。

11
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:35.000
and that may indeed be a protective factor. Part two is the question, when this is over and this two will pass, what characteristics matter? Who recovers? Who is a better leader? Who is more productive? And there, the data are that being cheerful and merry doesn't do anything at all. It's being optimistic and hopeful.
这确实可能是一个保护因素。第二部分是这个问题：当这一切结束时，而且这一切终将过去，什么特征才重要？谁会恢复？谁是更好的领导者？谁更有效率？在这些方面，数据表明，开朗和快乐根本不起作用。起作用的是乐观和希望。

12
00:07:35.000 --> 00:08:27.000
It's the people who interpret the future as they're going to be good events, bad events are going to fade, bad events are only just this one situation, and I can do something about it. These are the people who go on to be more productive, healthier, and better leaders. So the bottom line here is during the pandemic, have a great time as best you can. After the pandemic, and as it ends, it's hope and optimism that are going to predict recovery, leadership, and our future. That leads me to wonder a little bit about, you might call it stickiness. If people are not naturally optimistic, but they go through the actions of trying to behave in an optimistic way, does that?
正是那些将未来解读为会有好事发生、坏事会消退、坏事只是这一种情况、并且我可以做点什么的人，才会成为更有效率、更健康、更好的领导者。所以这里的底线是：在疫情期间，尽你所能享受美好时光。在疫情之后，随着它结束，预测恢复、领导力和我们未来的将是希望和乐观。这让我有点好奇，你可以称之为“粘性”。如果人们天生不乐观，但他们通过行动试图以乐观的方式行事，这会吗？

13
00:08:27.000 --> 00:09:14.000
measurably change their personality. Can you learn to be optimistic if that's not your natural bent? Yeah. And I'm a good example of that. So I'm naturally a depressive and a pessimist. And I take my own medicine and everything I'm about to say, I do and my family does. So when we have a good idea about an intervention in positive psychology, I first do it on myself. If it works on me, I give it to my wife and seven children to do. If it works on them, I give it to my graduate students and postdocs, and we start to do outcome studies on it. So one thing we have learned is that pessimism is changeable. You can change it to optimism.
可衡量地改变他们的性格吗？如果这不是你的天性，你能学会乐观吗？是的。我就是一个很好的例子。我天生就是一个抑郁症患者和悲观主义者。我服用自己的药，我即将说的每一件事，我和我的家人都在做。所以当我们对积极心理学中的某种干预措施有了一个好主意时，我首先在自己身上做实验。如果它对我有效，我就让我的妻子和七个孩子去做。如果对他们有效，我就让我的研究生和博士后去做，然后我们开始对其进行结果研究。所以我们学到的一件事是，悲观是可以改变的。你可以把它变成乐观。

14
00:09:14.000 --> 00:10:11.000
And the key to this is first tuning in to the most pessimistic. and the most catastrophic thoughts you say to yourself. And that's something people are pretty good at doing. It's the main move in cognitive behavioral therapy. And then having identified your most catastrophic thoughts, treat them as if they were said to you by someone whose mission in life was to make you miserable and argue rationally against them. So let me give you an example that I use, and I've used. used during this pandemic. So my first thought when I came back home after 17 days of isolation was, you know, this medical stuff
而关键在于首先要调频到你对自己说的最悲观、最灾难性的想法。这是人们非常擅长做的事情。这是认知行为疗法中的主要动作。然后，在识别出你最灾难性的想法后，把它们当作是一个人生使命就是让你痛苦的人对你说的，并理性地反驳它们。让我给你一个我在这次疫情期间使用过的例子。所以，在17天的隔离后回到家，我的第一个想法是，你知道，这些医疗的东西

15
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:41.000
is nonsense. They're still infectious. I'm going to be down with this in four days. And I'm 78 years old. So I'm dead. And the statistics are indeed that compared to the young population, I'm 222 times more likely to die. So that's my first thought, okay, my most catastrophic thought. And then I argued against it rationally.
都是胡扯。他们仍然具有传染性。我四天内就会倒下。而且我已经78岁了。所以我死定了。统计数据确实显示，与年轻群体相比，我死亡的可能性高出222倍。所以这是我的第一个想法，好吧，我最灾难性的想法。然后我理性地反驳了它。

16
00:10:41.000 --> 00:11:49.000
So what I said to myself is called the put it in perspective exercise. That says first go through the most catastrophic scenario possible. Okay? I just did it. The second part is go through the least catastrophic. What's the best thing that could happen? And so I said to myself, well, the best thing that can happen is they're not infectious. And indeed, these two wonderful vaccines are out. And I'm going to be just fine. And I'll get a vaccine first thing in January. Okay. Then the third thing to do is having done the worst possible scenario and the best possible scenario, what's the most realistic scenario? Well, the most realistic scenario is I'm going to be very limited between now and the time a jab occurs. And I'm not going to get this in January. You know, the president and the vice president got it yesterday, but I'm not going to get it till the spring. So what I have to do is plan for the future.
所以我对自己说的是所谓的“换位思考练习”。首先要经历最灾难性的可能情景。好吧？我刚刚做到了。第二部分是经历最不灾难性的情景。可能发生的最好的事情是什么？所以我对自己说，嗯，可能发生的最好的事情是他们没有传染性。而且确实，这两种很棒的疫苗已经出来了。我会没事的。我会在一月份第一时间接种疫苗。好吧。然后第三件事是，在做完了最坏和最好的情景后，最现实的情景是什么？嗯，最现实的情景是，从现在到接种疫苗之前，我的活动将受到很大限制。而且我不会在一月份就打上疫苗。你知道，总统和副总统昨天打了，但我得等到春天。所以我必须为未来做计划。

17
00:11:49.000 --> 00:12:58.000
I have to find a way to be at home most of the time to teach my classes and to minimize my exposure. So the most likely scenario is the next three months are going to be difficult and there is a pretty good chance if I'm careful I'll be able to avoid the infection. that's my early morning stuffiness. The catastrophic interpretation would be, there it is, I've got COVID. The most realistic explanation is early morning stuffiness. That's a good exercise that any one of our listeners could do and could benefit from. Let me change gears and talk a little bit about the effects of the pandemic on children and how disruptions to school and socializing and other life experiences may affect them in the long run. What kind of advice might you have for parents, educators, or others on how to help children's well-being right now? What can we do to help make them more optimistic?
我必须找到一种方法，大部分时间待在家里教课，并尽量减少接触。所以最可能的情景是接下来的三个月会很困难，如果我小心的话，我有很大机会避免感染。这就是我清晨的鼻塞。灾难性的解读会是：看吧，我感染新冠了。最现实的解释是清晨鼻塞。这是一个任何听众都可以做并从中受益的好练习。让我换个话题，谈谈疫情对儿童的影响，以及学校和社交的中断以及其他生活经历如何从长远上影响他们。对于父母、教育工作者或其他帮助儿童的人，你有什么建议来帮助他们现在的幸福感？我们能做些什么来帮助他们变得更乐观？

18
00:12:58.000 --> 00:14:00.000
Well, the same things work with children as working with adults, and that is recognizing catastrophic thoughts and disputing them. But there's no doubt that a year away from, from school and away from friends is going to make things more difficult. And so there, it's optimism that's going to matter and what we do when we come out of a pandemic. But I think I want to say something general, Kim, about my view of the future given the pandemic. I think the world is in labor and something is being born and that we will learn from this pandemic. what to keep and what to throw away. But importantly, there are two views of children and our future, given this pandemic. I'm going to call it Yates versus Juliana. Yates, 100 years ago,
嗯，对儿童有效的方法和对成年人一样，那就是识别灾难性想法并反驳它们。但毫无疑问，远离学校和朋友一年会让事情变得更困难。所以，乐观精神将是重要的，以及我们在疫情结束后要做什么。但我认为我想说一些关于我对疫情下未来的普遍看法，金。我认为世界正在阵痛中，有什么东西正在诞生，我们将从这次疫情中学习到什么该保留，什么该抛弃。但重要的是，鉴于这次疫情，关于儿童和我们的未来有两种观点。我称之为“叶芝”与“朱利安”。叶芝，100年前，

19
00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:48.000
in this poem about the troubled, asked what rough beast is slouching its way to Bethlehem now to be born? This is the pessimistic view that the labor that the world is in now is going to result in something worse than what we have. So the Yates view, highly pessimistic. And all you need to do is pick up the New York Times to hear the Yates view one way or another. Juliana of Norwich is the one I lean to. Juliana, as you may remember, was a monk. You had to take a male name to be a monk. She's called Julian of Norwich.
在他那首关于困境的诗中，问道：“是什么粗野的野兽，正蹒跚着向伯利恒走去，等待出生？”这是悲观的观点，认为世界现在所处的阵痛将导致比我们现在更糟糕的结果。所以叶芝的观点是非常悲观的。你只需要拿起《纽约时报》，就能以某种方式听到叶芝的观点。诺里奇的朱利安是我倾向的观点。朱利安，你可能记得，是一位修道士。你必须取一个男性名字才能成为修道士。她被称为诺里奇的朱利安。

20
00:14:48.000 --> 00:15:23.000
And she wrote in the middle of the Black Plague. And by the way, the Black Plague is a hundred times worse than what we're going through now. Humans have gone through plagues for time immemorial. The Black Plague killed one third of the European population. There was no safety net at all. There was no zooming. And it was in many ways the worst of times. And Juliana Neoplatonist mystic wrote the following about what was being born.
她写于黑死病期间。顺便说一句，黑死病比我们现在经历的要严重一百倍。人类从远古时代起就经历过瘟疫。黑死病杀死了欧洲三分之一的人口。当时根本没有任何安全网。没有Zoom。在许多方面，那是“最糟糕的时代”。而新柏拉图主义神秘主义者朱利安写下了关于正在诞生之物的以下内容。

21
00:15:23.000 --> 00:16:12.000
He said not, thou shalt not be travailed. He said not, thou shalt not be tempestiff. He said not, there shall not be diseased. He said, thou shalt not be overcome, and all shall be well, and all shall be well, and all manner of things shall be well. This is the dilemma we're in now about our future. Will there be a beast slouching its way to Bethlehem to be born to take over the world? or will all things be well? And for me, the Giuliana view and the Yates view are both self-fulfilling.
他说，不是，你不会受苦。他说，不是，你不会经历风暴。他说，不是，你不会生病。他说，你不会被战胜，一切都会好起来，一切都会好起来，各种各样的事情都会好起来。这就是我们现在关于未来的困境。会有一只野兽蹒跚着向伯利恒走去，准备接管世界吗？还是说一切都会好起来？对我来说，朱利安的观点和叶芝的观点都是自我实现的。

22
00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:48.000
So what we need here is optimism, planning, and hope for the future. Let's talk for a moment about some of your earliest research into learned helplessness. You studied dogs and other mammals. And I'm wondering how in the intervening years you've changed your thinking around learned helplessness. and now you talk more about the idea of learned hopefulness. Can you explain for our listeners first what is meant by learned helplessness, which they might not be familiar with, and then tell us how and why you're thinking and research in this area evolved?
所以我们这里需要的是乐观、规划和对未来的希望。让我们谈谈你早期关于“习得性无助”的研究。你研究了狗和其他哺乳动物。我想知道在随后的几年里，你是如何改变对习得性无助的看法的，现在你更多地谈论“习得性希望”的概念。你能先为我们的听众解释一下什么是习得性无助吗？他们可能不太熟悉，然后告诉我们你的想法和研究在这个领域是如何演变的？

