we began to get pretty good tools for looking at the brain. And Steve wanted to know what was happening in the brain of rats when they became helpless. And the findings were astonishing. And they flipped the field on its head. What Steve found is that both in animals and human, there's a structure sort of up the nose. It's 50,000 cells in a rat and 150,000 cells. in a human called the dorsal rafé nucleus. And the dorsal rafay nucleus, its activity produces helplessness.
