00:21:34.000 --> 00:22:34.000
And in fact, I think the best paper I ever wrote, which no one has read, is called What is a Dream? And for me, this nails the process of what dreaming is. But without going through the theory of dreaming, here was the dream. I'm walking up the ramp at the Guggenheim Museum, and I notice that on the right there are doors, and I open each of the doors, and there are people playing with cards. And I'm very puzzled about this. So I asked the question, why is everyone playing with cards, whereupon the roof of the Guggenheim opens and the godhead appears? And God, Kim, you'll be happy to hear, is an elderly male, look white beard, gray, with a booming voice. And God says to me, Seligman, at least you're starting to ask the right questions.
事实上，我认为我写过的最好的论文，虽然没人读过，叫做《什么是梦？》。对我来说，这揭示了做梦的过程。但如果不深入探讨做梦的理论，梦的内容是这样的：我正走在古根海姆博物馆的坡道上，我注意到右边有门，我打开每一扇门，里面有人在玩牌。我对此感到非常困惑。所以我问了一个问题：“为什么每个人都在玩牌？”随即古根海姆的屋顶打开了，上帝出现了。上帝，金，你会很高兴听到，是一位年长的男性，留着白胡子，灰色的，声音洪亮。上帝对我说：“塞利格曼，至少你开始问正确的问题了。”

31
00:22:34.000 --> 00:23:16.000
And that changed my life, actually. So it's what moved me from being an experimental psychologist to a clinical psychologist. from a experimental researcher to a longitudinal researcher and from animals to humans. So it's a dream that moved me and has stayed with me. Fast forward now 30 years. I'm elected president of the American Psychological Association, and I'm told that presidents are supposed to have initiatives. and I didn't really know what mine was going to be.
这确实改变了我的生活。这就是让我从实验心理学家转变为临床心理学家的原因。从实验研究者转变为纵向研究者，从动物研究转变为人类研究。所以这是一个触动了我并一直伴随着我的梦。现在快进30年。我被选为美国心理学会主席，有人告诉我总统应该有倡议，但我真的不知道我的会是什么。

